package typeck

// Package typeck is the type checker, a port of the Go compiler's
// package typeck: so far its declaration phase, everything Go's
// Checker.Check does before it checks the first function body
// (check.go:557-603), and the bodies (check.go:604-618, see body.tck),
// their statements and expressions, the calls in them and the values of
// lists, maps, arenas, pools, views, any and error they pass (see
// call.tck), the facts that prove an index in range, a divisor not zero
// and an error checked (see fact.tck), and their ownership: moves (see
// mover.tck), exclusivity and borrows (see exclusive.tck), view results
// (see viewresult.tck), the builtins that move values (see
// ownbuiltins.tck), regions and scope allocators (see scopealloc.tck)
// and escapes (see escape.tck), and its generics and closures: the
// bodies of generic functions, their calls, which infer the type
// arguments (see infer.tck), switches on a type parameter (see
// paramswitch.tck) and fields loops (see data.tck), function values
// (see funcvalue.tck) and closures (see closure.tck), and, once every
// body checks, the instances of generic functions the program needs
// (see instance.tck), as far as none needs a later stage.
// It takes a resolved program and the source files its spans point
// into, both taken over and given back, and gives every struct and union
// type its ID, its fields and its cases, every global its type and its
// constant value, every interface method and every function its
// signature, a generic function's type parameters included, and reports
// what Go reports of them, in Go's order: a type that contains itself,
// an element that cannot be copied, a key with no ==, a type too large,
// globals too large together, an initialization cycle, a global that is
// no constant, a type a declaration exposes, two extern funcs of one C
// name. Then it checks every body, every extern func's C side, the bad
// declarations and the declarations nothing uses, its errors, and then
// its warnings, given back by take_diags.

// The types are added to a types.Table in Go's order, so each gets the
// ID Go gives it: what the --port-types dump shows, which tackc's
// porttest compares with Go's checker run in process. What the checker
// finds is kept beside the tree, in maps keyed by node (see Checker),
// rather than written into nodes as Go writes it.
//
// A body is checked with the facts Go keeps beside it, put in force as
// conditions, loops, cases and early exits give them and taken out of
// force as their regions end, and with the relations in force, which a
// proof asks when a value's own bounds fall short (see relgraph.tck).
// Every construct that would need a later stage is declined before it
// could make a difference (see body.tck), so a body that checks is one
// whose every report is the port's too. Once a body reports nothing,
// its moves are followed, and then its escapes, as Go's checkFunc does.
// check_types keeps what the
// declaration phase found when only a body is declined, for
// --port-types, whose dump says how far the checker got.
//
// What the declaration phase meets that only a body needs, or that Go
// reports in words the port does not write yet, check declines,
// returning not_ported, which the driver reports as not implemented. So
// does every judgment it cannot make yet: before bodies a constant's
// operands are all known values, so an overflow, a divisor, an index or
// a conversion it cannot decide follows an error Go may follow with one
// of its own; and an ensures clause whose operands give facts it cannot
// tell from their shape alone (see ensures.tck). Nothing is taken as
// holding unproven, and the dump's assumed list stays empty.
//
// Go checks a global the first time it is needed, from inside the check
// of the global that names it, and a chain of globals nests a check for
// each link. Here only a few checks nest on the stack; past that the one
// needed is suspended and checked from a loop that keeps its own stack,
// and every check unwound is checked again from its start, what it did
// before staying done and what it reported staying reported (see
// check_globals). Every other walk along a chain the source can make as
// long as it likes, of operators, of fields and elements, of structs
// held by value, keeps its own stack too, so a program of any length
// checks within a bounded stack.
//
// # Stack discipline
//
// The parser lets an expression nest about a thousand levels deep, and
// the port must check every such input within a 4 MB stack, so a level
// of nesting, whatever construct it is, a call's argument, an element, a
// ?, an operator, may cost less than 4 KB of frames in all. The checks
// keep to that by construction: a chain of operators, of fields and
// elements, of method calls and of ?s is a spine, walked in a loop, and
// a binary operator's right operand is a spine of its own pushed on
// body_expr's stack (see expr), so neither recurses at all; a value
// nests in another through a few small frames, the check's work before
// and after its part done by functions of their own, which return
// before the part is checked (see field_key, question, dynamic_callee,
// find_method and method_args); and a fold or a walk of the moves keeps
// a stack of its own (see fold_run and mv_walk). The facts keep to it
// too: a condition's facts are worked out from a stack of frames (see
// facts_from), a region's writes, whether a block leaves and the jumps
// out of it from stacks of their own (see walk_writes, block_leaves and
// jumps_of), an else-if chain's links in a loop (see check_if), and the
// right side of an && or || costs only an entry on body_expr's stack of
// spines and one on the facts' (see logic_start). A generic call's
// argument is checked from check_written_args, not through check_arg's,
// check_arg_special's or infer_arg's frames (see infer_step), a value
// passed as any from check_written_args, out of check_arg's frame, or
// from check_arg, out of check_arg_special's (see any_plain), what
// checking a closure's body sets apart is kept on the Checker's stacks,
// not in a frame (see
// enter_closure), and an instance's body, a fields loop's and a case's
// are walked from a stack of their own (see inst_walk). clang inlines a
// function called from one place into its caller, frame and all, so a
// function on the path a level of nesting takes is kept small by giving
// what it calls only when it does not nest a call of its own, or does
// so rarely, a frame of its own: a second caller does it. The generated C gives
// every temporary of a function a slot for the whole call, so what
// matters is what a frame on the recursion's path holds, which the
// structure above keeps small whatever clang inlines into it.
// porttest's TestCheckDeepPairs checks the budget, nesting every pair
// of constructs as deeply as the parser allows at 4 MB.
